WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.196 --> 00:00:24.305
"Laurie, I got you something to celebrate your first horseback riding lesson," Grandpa announced, meeting Laurie at the

2
00:00:24.345 --> 00:00:25.225
top of the stairs.

3
00:00:26.386 --> 00:00:28.287
"Mongolian boots!"

4
00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:31.288
"Wow! They're pretty.

5
00:00:32.344 --> 00:00:33.865
But why Mongolian?"

6
00:00:34.226 --> 00:00:37.789
Laurie said, holding up the decorative orange and turquoise boots.

7
00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:42.833
"Because the Mongols have a history of being excellent horse riders!

8
00:00:44.274 --> 00:00:50.619
Back in the 1200s, when their empire was the mightiest in the world, they had riding skills even

9
00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:52.281
your instructor would envy.

10
00:00:53.281 --> 00:00:58.326
They could eat, sleep, and pick up an arrow from the ground without ever getting off their horses!"

11
00:00:59.507 --> 00:01:00.928
Laurie raised her eyebrows.

12
00:01:02.076 --> 00:01:07.281
"Not only that, but they have an amazing history too," Grandpa continued.

13
00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:09.563
"Would you like to hear a bit?"

14
00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:12.886
"Okay," Laurie answered.

15
00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:17.490
"Come on in," he said, and Laurie followed him into the study.

16
00:01:23.855 --> 00:01:28.820
Grandpa began, "is a country in Asia, between Russia and China.

17
00:01:29.884 --> 00:01:35.407
Its land is a mix of mountains, grasslands, and the driest desert in the world.

18
00:01:36.488 --> 00:01:41.791
Because Mongols live in such rough environments, they have become pretty tough themselves.

19
00:01:43.292 --> 00:01:46.474
But they love their land and feel deeply connected to it.

20
00:01:47.575 --> 00:01:54.018
The Mongols, you know, believe their people were created when a blue wolf and a female deer traveled

21
00:01:54.058 --> 00:01:56.240
to a sacred mountain in Mongolia.

22
00:01:57.364 --> 00:02:01.906
There the two animals mated and the deer later gave birth to a human.

23
00:02:02.966 --> 00:02:06.368
This human was the first ancestor of the Mongols."

24
00:02:07.568 --> 00:02:12.170
Grandpa paused to lift a furry hat off the bookshelf and place it on his head.

25
00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:22.295
"The early Mongols," he continued, "were nomads who lived in tents and migrated seasonally with their large herds

26
00:02:22.335 --> 00:02:24.956
of sheep, camels, yaks, and horses.

27
00:02:26.192 --> 00:02:28.755
In summer they lived in the high steppes.

28
00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:35.342
In winter they traveled to warmer, lower lands, in search of new grass for their animals.

29
00:02:36.424 --> 00:02:41.389
To the Mongols, land was sacred, so they would not plow it or settle on it.

30
00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:47.556
Water was sacred, so they would not bathe in it or dirty it with any of their waste.

31
00:02:48.608 --> 00:02:54.110
And the sky was sacred, so they climbed to the tops of mountains to be closer to their

32
00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.510
greatest god, known as Eternal Blue Heaven.

33
00:02:58.691 --> 00:03:02.492
For many years the Mongols existed as separate tribes.

34
00:03:03.672 --> 00:03:08.994
Even though the Mongol tribes shared a common culture and way of life, they fought often.

35
00:03:11.154 --> 00:03:16.536
At one point the fighting was so intense, it looked like they might all destroy each other.

36
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.522
But then in the twelfth century, a great leader united them.

37
00:03:22.743 --> 00:03:26.486
He built a mighty empire that connected the East and the West forever."

38
00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:29.388
"Who?" Laurie blurted out.

39
00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:31.049
"Was he a Mongol?"

40
00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.112
"Yes. He was a Mongol named Temujin.

41
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:40.416
According to legend, he was born holding a blood clot in his fist.

42
00:03:41.377 --> 00:03:45.860
This was either a sign of danger or a sign that he would be a great warrior.

43
00:03:47.308 --> 00:03:50.471
Temujin turned out to be a strong, brave warrior.

44
00:03:51.612 --> 00:03:56.215
After many battles he became the khan, or leader, of his tribe.

45
00:03:57.376 --> 00:04:05.263
Once he had conquered all the neighboring tribes, he was called Genghis Khan, which means oceanic or universal

46
00:04:05.343 --> 00:04:13.910
leader. Under Genghis Khan's leadership, the Mongol Empire expanded to include most of Asia, much of the Middle

47
00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.752
East, and a large part of modern-day Russia.

48
00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:21.558
It was the largest empire ever established by one man."

49
00:04:21.578 --> 00:04:25.279
"Wow. Really?"

50
00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:26.500
Laurie remarked.

51
00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.501
"What made him such a great leader?"

52
00:04:29.521 --> 00:04:38.805
"Well, Genghis Khan understood what is needed to keep a stable empire: a common written language, law and

53
00:04:39.005 --> 00:04:42.746
order, good communication, and a strong, loyal army.

54
00:04:43.687 --> 00:04:46.508
He made sure the Mongols had all these things.

55
00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:53.286
The Mongols never had a written language, so he chose the alphabet of one of the conquered tribes

56
00:04:53.686 --> 00:04:56.008
and made it the official Mongolian script.

57
00:04:57.429 --> 00:05:03.675
Then he ordered the Mongolian cultural and social rules to be written in a book and demanded that

58
00:05:03.795 --> 00:05:05.857
people be punished if they disobeyed them.

59
00:05:07.078 --> 00:05:14.996
And—at a time when there were no postal systems—he built a messenger system that could quickly carry messages

60
00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:17.598
on horseback across long distances.

61
00:05:18.779 --> 00:05:22.522
But perhaps his greatest ideas had to do with the army.

62
00:05:23.983 --> 00:05:29.567
He separated soldiers from their fellow tribesmen so that they would be more loyal to him than to

63
00:05:29.607 --> 00:05:30.688
their fellow soldiers.

64
00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:36.072
And he promoted them based on their skill rather than their social position.

65
00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.662
This was a revolutionary idea at the time.

66
00:05:40.723 --> 00:05:44.465
Genghis Khan also had the reputation of being one of his people.

67
00:05:45.606 --> 00:05:51.030
That means he wore the same clothes they wore, ate the same food they ate, and rode the

68
00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:52.531
same horses they rode.

69
00:05:54.052 --> 00:05:56.694
He didn't treat himself like a king or an emperor.

70
00:05:57.435 --> 00:05:59.016
This made him popular.

71
00:05:59.416 --> 00:06:04.100
When he died in 1227, a great funeral was held for him.

72
00:06:05.184 --> 00:06:10.008
He was buried at the mountain where the blue wolf and the deer created the first Mongol so

73
00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:10.949
many years ago.

74
00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:18.976
But the exact location has never been found because after the funeral, eight hundred horsemen rode over the

75
00:06:19.036 --> 00:06:21.878
spot to destroy any sign of a grave."

76
00:06:21.898 --> 00:06:27.243
"Whoa Eight hundred horseback riders?"

77
00:06:27.663 --> 00:06:28.444
Laurie asked.

78
00:06:29.628 --> 00:06:36.172
"Well, important men get impressive funerals, and Genghis Khan was a very important Mongol.

79
00:06:37.373 --> 00:06:42.356
Under his leadership, the Mongol Empire left a huge impact on the world.

80
00:06:43.556 --> 00:06:48.859
After being abandoned for some time, the Silk Road reopened under Genghis Khan's rule.

81
00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:56.484
The Mongols helped promote the transfer of goods, ideas, and culture between Asia and Europe.

82
00:06:57.536 --> 00:07:04.061
The Silk Road brought so many interesting items from the East to the West that Westerners became extremely

83
00:07:04.261 --> 00:07:05.562
curious about Asia.

84
00:07:06.742 --> 00:07:12.887
In the late thirteenth century, the famous Italian explorer Marco Polo traveled to China.

85
00:07:13.987 --> 00:07:20.632
He lived in the palace of Kublai Khan, Genghis Khan's grandson who had become the emperor of China.

86
00:07:22.748 --> 00:07:29.734
Marco Polo's adventures inspired Christopher Columbus to sail around the world in search of a faster route to

87
00:07:29.854 --> 00:07:34.838
Asia. I'd say the Mongol Empire was pretty important, wouldn't you?"

88
00:07:34.858 --> 00:07:38.160
"Yes." Laurie pulled on the boots.

89
00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:42.764
"But more importantly, these boots are beautiful.

90
00:07:43.885 --> 00:07:45.906
I hope Mom lets me wear them to school.

91
00:07:46.927 --> 00:07:47.868
Thanks, Grandpa!

92
00:07:49.741 --> 00:07:52.661
Giddyap!" she cried, galloping out of the study.
